  "baseRefOid": "abdc6a7002abdbcd9e21bfc5e1a795784deacfdf",
  "body": "The historical quadratic-code rank checker rebuilds large XOR-permutation masks for every row. This **tooling improvement** adds an opt-in per-replay mask cache: the complete supported `t=6`, 4,096-row replay takes **87.66 s \u2192 18.36 s (4.78\u00d7)** on macOS arm64 / CPython 3.14.6. These are medians of three alternating fresh-process pairs on identical inputs; all six complete receipts are byte-identical.\n\nOnly `research/cube-xor-replay/` and its focused Linux workflow are added. The original source, row order, elimination, stopping rule, certificates, selected frontier and `upstream/` remain unchanged. No new \u03ba or impossibility result: the `t=6` rank lower bound is 3,088 and positive deficit remains **not excluded**.\n\n### Reproduce\n\n```sh\npython3 -I -O -B research/cube-xor-replay/verify.py\n```\n\nExpected: `PASS_CUBE_XOR_VERIFICATION rows=4324 coefficients=8596927616 t6_rank=3088 t6_excluded=false`.\n\nFor the accelerated workload alone: `python3 -I -B research/cube-xor-replay/replay.py --case t6`. Python 3.10+, standard library only. Full differential verification deliberately also executes the original permutation and therefore is slower than the accelerated replay.\n\n### Evidence and scope\n\n- Direct equality of every generated row, including dependent rows: **4,324 rows / 8,596,927,616 binary coefficients**. The complete historical `t=3,4,5` certificate is also unchanged.\n- Algebraic equivalence argument for every valid power-of-two row width. Only geometry masks are cached; input guards still run on warm hits. No row/rank/admission result cache.\n- **17 tests** pass under optimized Python 3.14.6 and 3.12.12, including exhaustive small rows, all small basis/index pairs, seed-94 dense controls, high coordinates, incomplete screens, source/fixture/manifest corruption, and offline relocation.\n- A common column permutation preserves rank and independent-row indices but changes the witness stream; complete receipt comparison rejects it. Dropped rows and corrupt masks are detected.\n- All **nine unchanged cube tests** pass with the cached backend; selected-record integrity and its five tests pass. All **38 source Git/SHA256 pins** match.\n- `BENCHMARK.json` retains six raw samples, and `VALIDATION.json` records commands and code hashes. Full `make verify`, selected native cold replay, Lean and hosted CI success are not claimed. This does not measure selected-frontier or integer-multiplication runtime.\n\nResearch base: `2921874911956718fcaaaf9396fe8267b3cfa9f0`. Submission refresh: `abdc6a7002abdbcd9e21bfc5e1a795784deacfdf`; all pinned inputs and PR942's selected `1661960547713947/10^18` are unchanged. Historical source: `d55bcd62ceb8f322bcf311a5a66e94c3ac7724f0`.\n\nOS seed **94**, verification-engineering track. `CLAIM.json` records three candidates and scope. There was no pinned coordination issue. Open work was refreshed through #1072; exact XOR-permutation searches found no duplicate. PR667 logarithms, PR752 quadratic-affine products, PR898 Leech lanes and PR976 joint-frame rank are distinct and credited; no code from those PRs is imported. The bounded `t=6` workload uses the existing default cap, not a newly extended construction search.\n\nCredit Douglas R. Colkitt for the original family, permutation, audit and certificate. Contribution by **Ross Richardson with substantial OpenAI Codex assistance**, including implementation, analysis, testing and documentation; no independent human review claimed. Apache-2.0. All inherited mathematical/compiler/hosting/precision/tape/analytic assumptions retain their scope.\n",
